    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d537">

                <pb facs="0967" n="863" sameAs="frus:frus1943CairoTehran/pg863"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">Roosevelt
                    Papers: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                    >Winant</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the President</hi><anchor n="1" xml:id="d537-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >London</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1944-01-03">3 January 1944</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>To the President from Winant.</p>
                <p>After reading the Prime Minister’s number 530 to you, I wanted you to know that a
                    message from Mister Hull instructed me to
                    take up the question of what was said in relation to unconditional surrender at
                    Teheran with the Prime Minister on his return to London<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d537-f2"/>… I hope the Prime Minister’s query to you was in a form
                    acceptable to you. Eden meant it to be so
                    and the Prime Minister followed his suggestion in his cable to you. Eden thought that the subject had come up at a
                    luncheon conversation at the Russian Embassy. There has been no further word
                    from Stalin.</p>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . . . . . . . . .</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
